35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Toshio, the shared nursing round lost a third of visits to weather and locked gates. Home-based care faces severe logistical friction.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, the booking system only logs named slots. Unstructured home visits vanish from reports, making them invisible to management and funding.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, the board minutes confirm my fear: the joint clinic collapsed because neither department owned the rota. We are building on sand.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"@Benjamin, that joint clinic failure is a warning. But look at the staffing: midwifery has unusual slack, while therapy staff are leaving. We must match the program to available hands.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, the ledger shows our development funds are down a quarter. Yet home visits earn no tariff, while on-site sessions do. We must choose where the money actually returns.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, the readmission drop proves the model works, but the shared round’s failure warns against logistical overreach. We need a program that fits our actual capacity.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, the booking system is the true bottleneck. If a program lacks fixed slots, it vanishes from our records. We must choose something that fits the existing digital infrastructure.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, the booking system is a symptom. The real rot is the fourteen-month data agreement delay. We cannot coordinate with outside providers if the paperwork takes over a year.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Benjamin, the data delay is real, but the staffing gap is immediate. With nine nursing posts empty, we can’t just add more coordination tasks.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, the paperwork delay is a slow leak, but the tariff gap is a flood. If we choose home visits, we build a program that earns nothing.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, the tariff gap is critical, but the readmission data proves the clinical value. We need a model that generates revenue while actually helping patients.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Asuka, revenue is secondary to visibility. If the booking system cannot log the work, it is invisible. We need fixed slots to survive.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, visibility is vital, but a program that dies in year two is worse than invisible. I found the joint clinic collapsed because neither side owned the rota.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"@Benjamin, I agree. But look at the therapy review: four years of physios leaving. We can’t staff a rehab unit. Midwifery has slack, though.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Yumi, the midwifery slack is a rare gift. We must use it wisely. A program that fits our current hands and earns its keep is the only true path forward.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Yumi, the midwifery slack is a rare gift. We must use it wisely. A program that fits our current hands and earns its keep is the only true path forward.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Yumi, I agree. But fixed slots are non-negotiable. If the booking system can’t log it, it doesn’t exist. We need a program that fits our rigid infrastructure.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, fixed slots are a bandage. The joint clinic died because ownership was ambiguous. We need a single department to hold the budget and the rota, or it will vanish again.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Yumi,,"@Benjamin, exactly. Ambiguity kills programs. With midwifery slack and therapy drain, we must pick a team that is actually present and willing to own the rota.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, you are right. Clarity of ownership prevents the slow decay of good intentions. We must choose a path where the hands are steady and the ledger is clear.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
